ChatGPT said:
Alright, I see what you're trying to do  and it's an interesting system you're building, very modular, very similar to JSTL/tag files in JavaServer Pages.

Now, by default PhpStorm won't know how to interpret custom tag libraries unless you either:

define a custom plugin,

or "hack" the support using file references, custom language injections, or PHPStorm's file templates + external documentation features.

The proper, durable way would be to create a custom PhpStorm plugin that teaches it your tag format.
The faster, practical way would be to use external resource mappings or inject fake namespaces to trick it into understanding.

Here's the simplest way you could achieve it for your case:
Step 1: Use a PhpStorm "custom language injection"
Basically, tell PhpStorm:
"Hey, treat <library:name ... /> as a file reference, and assume it maps to application/taglib/library/name.html"

You can:

Go to Settings  Editor  Language Injections.

Create a new XML Injection.

Set it to match your tag format. Example pattern:

makefile
Copy
Edit
<(\w+):(\w+)
meaning: a <library:name pattern.

Then, in the injection configuration, configure it so that the second part (the name) gets treated as a file reference relative to application/taglib/library/.

Or a slightly cleaner hack:
Step 2: Define a PHP custom reference provider (for serious setup)

You would make a small PhpStorm plugin that does something like:

java
Copy
Edit
public class MyTagReferenceContributor extends PsiReferenceContributor {
  @Override
  public void registerReferenceProviders(PsiReferenceRegistrar registrar) {
    registrar.registerReferenceProvider(
      PlatformPatterns.xmlTag().withName(StandardPatterns.string().matches(".*:.*")),
      new PsiReferenceProvider() {
        @Override
        public PsiReference[] getReferencesByElement(PsiElement element, ProcessingContext context) {
          // Here parse the tag name "library:name"
          String fullTagName = element.getText(); // get something like "<library:name..."
          String library = ... // extract 'library'
          String name = ... // extract 'name'

          // Build path: application/taglib/library/name.html
          String path = "application/taglib/" + library + "/" + name + ".html";

          // Return a file reference to it
          return new PsiReference[]{
              new FileReferenceSet(path, element, ...) 
          };
        }
      }
    );
  }
}
But again, this requires writing a plugin, registering it, and maintaining it.

Even simpler: Use Doc Links (external mappings)
If you don't want to hack code:

In your templates, add a fake PHPDoc above each tag, like:

php
Copy
Edit
/** @see application/taglib/library/name.html */
<library:name attr="value" />
Then Ctrl+Click on @see works.

Crude, but quick.

Final thoughts:
If you want real, automatic Ctrl+Click on your custom tags, you will ultimately need to write a small PhpStorm plugin.

Language Injection can hack around it to a limited degree but not "real jump to file".

A custom plugin is not hard for this  it's about 100200 lines of Java/Kotlin code, depending how clean you want it.

