You said:
I'm downloading the newest phpstorm version. Do I need to uninstall the old one?


ChatGPT said:
Good move  going to the latest PhpStorm will definitely avoid lots of pain. 

Short answer:
No, you dont need to uninstall the old PhpStorm manually.

 PhpStorm versions install side-by-side by default.
 You can have multiple versions installed at once (e.g., 2023.1, 2023.3, 2024.1).
 Each has its own config folder and own installation directory.

 Here's what happens:
When you install the new PhpStorm (e.g., 2024.1), it will ask you:

"Do you want to import settings from an old version?"

You can:

Say YES  it copies your settings, keymaps, plugins, etc. from the old one.

Or say NO  fresh install.

 I recommend:

If you're just testing plugin dev, you can say NO (fresh start is clean).

Otherwise, say YES if you want all your color schemes, keybindings, etc.

 After installing the new PhpStorm:
Just open it.

Open your plugin project inside it.

Build normally with:

bash
Copy
Edit
./gradlew buildPlugin
 No conflict.
 Old PhpStorm version still installed (you can delete it later if you want manually).

 Bonus Tip:
If you're doing plugin development, PhpStorm will auto-detect that you're working on a plugin and suggest running a sandbox:

"Do you want to run your plugin inside a new PhpStorm instance (IDE Sandbox)?"

This is amazing because:

It launches a temporary PhpStorm with your plugin loaded.

No need to install .zip manually every time!

Changes are safe  your real PhpStorm config remains untouched.

 TL;DR
No uninstall needed.

New PhpStorm will install alongside.

Import settings if you want (optional).

Build your plugin cleanly in the new version.

If you want, once you open the new PhpStorm,
I can guide you step-by-step to set up the plugin sandboxing for easy testing (no packaging every time).

 Would you like me to show you how?  (saves huge amounts of time once configured)







