belong to the IntelliJ Platform SDK,
not to plain Java SDK.

 When you compile manually using javac, the IntelliJ classes don't exist outside PhpStorm environment.

 In simple words:
Project and VirtualFile = part of IntelliJ Plugin Runtime, not standard Java.

The main() method you want is a pure Java test, no IntelliJ dependencies allowed.

 Solution:
We need to split ApplicationPathsService into two modes:


Mode	Code
Plugin Mode (inside PhpStorm)	Use Project and VirtualFile
Command-line Mode	Use simple File and String operations
 They must be separated.

 Heres the fixed clean split version:
New: ApplicationPathsReader.java (pure Java, no IntelliJ dependency)
java
Copy
Edit
package com.lucianpopescu.viewlanguage;

import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class ApplicationPathsReader {

    public static String extractPath(String xmlContent, String tag, String defaultPath) {
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("<" + tag + ">(.*?)</" + tag + ">");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(xmlContent);
        if (matcher.find()) {
            return matcher.group(1).trim();
        }
        return defaultPath;
    }

    public static ApplicationPaths readFromFile(File file) throws IOException {
        String content = new String(Files.readAllBytes(file.toPath()), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

        String taglib = extractPath(content, "tags", "application/taglib");
        String views = extractPath(content, "views", "application/views");

        return new ApplicationPaths(taglib, views);
    }

    public static class ApplicationPaths {
        public final String taglibPath;
        public final String viewsPath;

        public ApplicationPaths(String taglibPath, String viewsPath) {
            this.taglibPath = taglibPath;
            this.viewsPath = viewsPath;
        }
    }

    // Command line testing
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length != 1) {
            System.err.println("Usage: java ApplicationPathsReader <path_to_application_stdout.xml>");
            System.exit(1);
        }

        File file = new File(args[0]);
        if (!file.exists()) {
            System.err.println("File does not exist: " + file.getAbsolutePath());
            System.exit(1);
        }

        try {
            ApplicationPaths paths = readFromFile(file);

            System.out.println("Taglib Path: " + paths.taglibPath);
            System.out.println("Views Path: " + paths.viewsPath);

        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
 Explanation:
ApplicationPathsReader is pure Java, no IntelliJ classes.

You can compile it with javac and run it easily from the command line.

 It reads application_stdout.xml and prints <tags> and <views> values.

 How to compile and run:
bash
Copy
Edit
javac -d out src/main/java/com/lucianpopescu/viewlanguage/ApplicationPathsReader.java
java -cp out com.lucianpopescu.viewlanguage.ApplicationPathsReader /absolute/path/to/application_stdout.xml
 Meanwhile, inside your plugin, you should still keep ApplicationPathsService.java but modify it:
java
Copy
Edit
package com.lucianpopescu.viewlanguage;

import com.intellij.openapi.project.Project;
import com.intellij.openapi.vfs.VirtualFile;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class ApplicationPathsService {

    private static String taglibPath = "application/taglib";
    private static String viewsPath = "application/views";

    public static void initialize(Project project) {
        VirtualFile xmlFile = project.getBaseDir().findFileByRelativePath("xml/application_stdout.xml");
        if (xmlFile != null) {
            try {
                ApplicationPathsReader.ApplicationPaths paths = ApplicationPathsReader.readFromFile(
                    new java.io.File(xmlFile.getPath())
                );
                taglibPath = paths.taglibPath;
                viewsPath = paths.viewsPath;
            } catch (IOException ignored) {
            }
        }
    }

    public static String getTaglibPath() {
        return taglibPath;
    }

    public static String getViewsPath() {
        return viewsPath;
    }
}
 ApplicationPathsService now reuses the pure Java reader.

 Result:

Environment	What happens
Command-line	You can test ApplicationPathsReader
Inside plugin	You call ApplicationPathsService.initialize(project) as before
 Quick Summary:
ApplicationPathsReader = pure Java (for testing / reuse).

ApplicationPathsService = plugin adapter (IntelliJ-specific code).

Full separation = no compilation conflicts, full testing freedom.

 Next step for you:
